 ## Overview
-Inverse rendering recovers latent scene parameters—such as camera pose, light placement, and surface color—that produce a given target photograph by formulating the problem as an unconstrained optimization task using gradients from the rendering pipeline [S1]. This method specifically estimates the 3D position and intensity of a punctual light source by minimizing the photometric error between a rendered image and an observed RGBD image, using a completely differentiable renderer based on the Blinn-Phong model with cast shadows [S2, unofficial]. Unlike methods that use discrete candidate light sources or explicit shadow detection, this approach performs continuous optimization of light position and renders a shadow map at each iteration [S2, unofficial]. Unlike Neverova et al. [22], this method does not rely on explicit separation between specular and diffuse components, nor does it segment images or extract surface properties [S2, unofficial]. Builds on [Image-based reconstruction optimization](6677-image-based-reconstruction-optimization.md).
